<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Root VINTF *device* manifest for the recovery ramdisk.

  This file must exist for the FBE decrypt HALs to be declared at all.
  libvintf's VintfObject::fetchDeviceHalManifest() reads /vendor/etc/vintf/
  manifest.xml first and only calls addDirectoryManifests() on the
  /vendor/etc/vintf/manifest/ fragment directory when that root file parsed
  successfully. The base OrangeFox ramdisk ships no root manifest.xml, so the
  keymaster/gatekeeper fragment next to this file was silently never loaded:

    hwservicemanager: getTransport: Cannot find entry
      android.hardware.keymaster@4.0::IKeymasterDevice/default in either
      framework or device VINTF manifest.
    hwservicemanager: getTransport: Cannot find entry
      android.hardware.gatekeeper@1.0::IGatekeeper/default ...

  With no keymaster instance visible in the manifest, keystore2's
  shared_secret_negotiation finds zero participants and concludes trivially
  ("Shared secret negotiation concluded successfully." before it has even
  found the device), so computeSharedHmac() is never issued to the TA and the
  auth-token HMAC key is never established. Gatekeeper then compares the PIN
  correctly but cannot mint a usable auth token, and returns -24 to
  GatekeeperHalDevice::verify_legacy. TWRP logs that as "GateKeeper response
  error", keystore2 as "No suitable auth token found /
  KEY_USER_NOT_AUTHENTICATED", and PIN decrypt fails while no-credential
  decrypt (which needs no auth token) keeps working.

  Proven on-device 2026-07-26: with a wrong PIN the trustlet returns -30
  (VERIFICATION_FAILED) and with the correct PIN -24, i.e. the comparison
  itself always succeeded and only the post-match token minting failed.
  Dropping this file in and restarting hwservicemanager + the HAL services
  turned the -24 into a successful add_auth_token() and decrypted user 0.

  Note: once TWRP mounts the real vendor partition, this ramdisk file is
  shadowed by LG's own /vendor/etc/vintf/manifest.xml. That is harmless:
  hwservicemanager parses VINTF once at its own startup, long before recovery
  mounts anything, so this file is the one that counts (and LG's declares the
  same two HALs anyway).

  The fragment android.hardware.keymaster_gatekeeper_caymanslm.xml is kept
  alongside this file on purpose: OrangeFox locates the keymaster version to
  start by `find`ing a ramdisk manifest whose *filename* contains
  "android.hardware.keymaster" (that is what sets keymaster_ver=4.x). The
  duplicate declarations across the two files are tolerated by libvintf, and
  that is the exact combination proven on-device.
-->
<manifest version="1.0" type="device">
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.keymaster</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>4.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IKeymasterDevice</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.gatekeeper</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>1.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IGatekeeper</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
</manifest>
